A large task is no longer one agent. When the orchestrator marks a task as large, it goes in stages: an **author** plans it with the Design model, and once the user approves the plan a **new agent**, the **implementer**, builds it with the Implementation model, in the same worktree, branch and pull request. Only one agent of the task lives at a time. Small tasks keep one node, which may use subagents for those parts inside its own session.

## What changes

- **Stages chosen per task, with the reason visible.** The launch plan carries `stages: {staged, reason}`. The orchestrator decides by size; the task's detail shows the choice and its reason. A model the user chose by hand for the task holds in every stage; otherwise each role takes its profile from Settings. Each role's selection is kept apart from the task's own selection, so the existing rules against changing the model, account or provider of a started task stay as they are.
- **Role per execution.** Every execution of a task is recorded with its role, model, provider and account (`TaskStages.runs`, table `task_stages`). Tasks stored before this read as one execution with no role.
- **Plan approval is recorded and hands over.** Every approved plan is recorded on the task. For a staged task's author, the approval ends the author's turn instead of letting it build the plan: Claude is denied with a handover message, and Codex settles the plan without queuing a new turn. The implementer's first input is queued at the same moment, so the author is never woken to carry on by itself.
- **A new change of role.** `Kind::Stage` is a new provider-transition kind. It picks the implementer's account and model, then waits until the author rests with nothing pending (no turn, no work of its own, no unanswered question). It ends the author's host and starts a new session from a context Alondra writes (request, approved plan, the task's records, worktree, branch and PRs), never the author's conversation. The new session starts only once the gate has a slot for the task's next turn. A restart resumes the change from its journal, taking the author's host over first. After three failed tries, the user gets a card to try again or cancel.
- **Tools limited by role.** The author plans and never registers, presents or declares work after a PR. The implementer does everything but record a reviewer's verdict. The validator never plans, registers a PR or records a verdict. A refused call is `role_forbids_tool`, naming the tool and saying what the role does instead. A plan the role may not send, or the author's plan after its approval, is denied without a card.
- **The task shows each agent.** The task detail has a new "Stages" section on iPhone, Mac and the terminal: the orchestrator's choice and reason, the next role while a change is pending, and the role, model and cost of each execution. A model decision is recorded for each stage's launch, so the measures keep the model of every execution.
- **Instructions.** The orchestrator skill's "Split work that needs design" section becomes "Stages: one task, separate agents for large work". It no longer says to create three tasks; it says when to stage by size. "One task, one node" now allows one node per stage. The node skill explains each role and the refusal. For the parallel task-splitting work, only that section of the orchestrator skill changed. The rest of its text is untouched.
- **State machines.** New section M-LIFE-15 (task stages), and `Kind::Stage` in M-ENGINE-7. `docs/architecture/task-states.md` explains a task in stages.

