At 16:9 the port narrows 2D pictures to keep their shape. A full-screen picture is now widened to fill the screen instead when its replacement is wider art:

1. 16:9 (and 'Stretch the 2D layer' off);
2. a 2D draw covering the whole screen in both directions;
3. the game's texture is 320 pixels wide or more (smaller full-screen textures are stretched already);
4. the replacement's width : height is at least 1.2 times the original's (4:3 art redrawn at 16:9 is 1.33).

The original content belongs in the middle three quarters; 2D elements drawn over it keep their places. Documented in `docs/modding.md`.

Known limit: the shape is decided before the draw's textures are bound, so the very first picture a new replacement appears in still has the old width. Pictures built from several tiles are not widened.

Tested: the 640x480 wrist-strap screen with a 854x480 replacement fills 16:9 and is squeezed into 4:3 at `--aspect 4:3`; `tools/mod_test.py` now checks that (and that same-shape art stays pillarboxed). `tools/smoke.py`, `mod_test.py`, self-tests OK; `tools/playthrough.py` passed 17/17 on this change before its rebase onto master.

